WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.025
You search a gigabyte log for one error string. Restarting after every mismatch is slow. KMP remembers what already matched.

00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:12.153
Pause and think.

00:00:12.553 --> 00:00:15.061
What does the KMP failure table store?

00:00:15.461 --> 00:00:22.776
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:23.975 --> 00:00:28.654
How do you find a pattern inside a long text without starting over after every mismatch?

00:00:30.004 --> 00:00:32.825
Imagine reading a song lyric and humming along.

00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:38.291
If you sang la la la and then made a mistake, you do not restart the whole song.

00:00:38.741 --> 00:00:43.779
You remember that the last la la is still the start of the chorus, and continue from there.

00:00:44.229 --> 00:00:48.653
KMP remembers that overlap in a small table called the failure function.

00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:53.242
Here is the failure table for the pattern A B A B C.

00:00:53.692 --> 00:00:59.311
Each entry is the length of the longest proper prefix that is also a suffix of the pattern up to that point.

00:00:59.761 --> 00:01:05.694
At index three, A B A B ends with A B, which is also its start, so the value is two.

00:01:06.144 --> 00:01:10.312
On a mismatch, jump the pattern pointer to that value instead of zero.

00:01:10.392 --> 00:01:18.751
New word: pointer. A variable holding the memory address of something else. Picture it like this: A note saying "the book is on shelf 4".

00:01:21.301 --> 00:01:25.237
Rolling hash, used by Rabin Karp, is the other classic.

00:01:25.687 --> 00:01:28.728
Turn each window into a number, like reading digits.

00:01:29.178 --> 00:01:34.264
Sliding by one removes the leftmost digit and adds a new one, in constant time.

00:01:34.714 --> 00:01:38.452
If hashes match, compare characters to rule out a rare collision.

00:01:39.802 --> 00:01:48.033
KMP never re-reads text thanks to the failure table; rolling hash compares windows as numbers. Both run in linear time.

00:01:48.034 --> 00:01:49.288
Back to the question.

00:01:49.688 --> 00:01:52.196
What does the KMP failure table store?

00:01:52.596 --> 00:01:56.868
For each position, the length of the longest prefix that is also a suffix.

00:01:58.068 --> 00:01:58.939
Quick recap.

00:01:59.339 --> 00:02:01.208
Never re-read matched text.

00:02:01.608 --> 00:02:03.442
Build the prefix table first.

00:02:03.842 --> 00:02:05.212
Linear time overall.
